# {{ project_name }} agent instructions

## Scope and sources of truth

- This file applies to the whole repository. Add a nearer `AGENTS.md` only when
  a subtree genuinely needs different commands, ownership, or safety rules.
- Treat this file as the entry point for AI instructions; `CLAUDE.md` only
  imports it, and task-specific details live in the linked repository docs.
- Treat `.csarc/profile.json` as the source of truth for enabled language and
  CI/CD modules. Use repository configuration for formatting, lint, type, test,
  and build rules.
- Use the linked Issue or `docs/specs/` for scope and acceptance criteria, and
  `docs/decisions/` for accepted architecture, tooling, security,
  compatibility, and platform decisions.
- Edit site content in `docs/site-content.js` and theme overrides in
  `docs/site-theme.css`, then rebuild `docs/index.html`; never hand-edit the
  generated bundle.

## Working loop

1. At the start of every task, run `./scripts/cleanup-worktrees` in its default
   dry-run mode and report any candidates; this startup safety net does not
   replace the creator's cleanup duty below.
2. Before planning or opening an Issue or Milestone, list open Milestones and
   open Issues, then search open and closed Issues with two to four concrete
   terms and at most 20 results per query. Inspect the body, comments, and linked
   pull requests of credible matches; titles and labels alone are not decisions.
3. When the user can respond, summarize prior decisions before creating work.
   When creation is already authorized, record each relevant `#N` as preserved,
   superseded, or rejected with a reason in `補充` or Milestone `References`;
   if none were found, record the bounded queries. Never store a raw conversation
   transcript or promote an unconfirmed inference. Never silently reverse an
   earlier decision.
4. Open or select one Issue before editing and keep its acceptance criteria as
   the scope boundary. Titles use 12-80 ASCII characters and at least three
   words, with no type prefix or trailing period. Issues use `類型`, `問題`,
   `完成條件`, and optional `補充`; pull requests use `Purpose`, `完成清單`,
   and optional `補充`, whether creating through the UI, CLI, or API.
5. {% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}Start from the Issue's delivery branch (`dev/m<milestone-number>-<slug>` or `dev/next`), or from an open work branch whose pull request chain ends there. Read `docs/ci-policy.md` before using an isolated `dev/i*`, hotfix, sync, promotion, canary, or release path.{% else %}Start from `{{ branch_strategy }}`, or from an open work branch whose pull request chain ends at `{{ branch_strategy }}`.{% endif %} Name normal branches `type/<issue-number>-short-slug`.
6. Inspect nearby code, tests, and configuration; make the smallest coherent
   change and preserve unrelated work and project-owned sources. If the scope
   grows, stop and open a separate Issue.
7. Add or update the narrowest regression test that proves non-trivial behavior.
   Run targeted checks while iterating and the required final check before a PR.
8. Before using `Closes`, `Fixes`, or `Resolves`, complete every task in the
   pull request and referenced Issue. {% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}Open an Issue PR against its delivery branch or immediate parent in the stack.{% else %}Open the PR against `{{ branch_strategy }}` or its immediate parent in the stack.{% endif %} Never merge it yourself unless the repository explicitly permits author self-merge and every applicable gate is satisfied.
9. After a PR is merged, its worktree creator must leave that worktree, fetch the
   integration branch, and run `./scripts/cleanup-worktrees --apply --worktree
   <path> origin/{% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}<delivery-branch>{% else %}{{ branch_strategy }}{% endif %}` from another checkout. For abandoned work, remove only a clean
   worktree you created and keep the branch. Never remove a platform-managed,
   dirty, current, locked, detached, unmerged, or unverifiable worktree.
10. Report changed behavior, verification evidence, and remaining limitations.

A Milestone is an optional, independently verifiable story outcome, not an
Issue-count bucket. Follow `docs/milestone-description.md` when planning one;
do not attach its PRs as duplicate progress. {% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}Keep its promotion Issue, or the tracking Issue for an isolated `dev/i*`, open until acceptance criteria and promotion evidence are complete.{% else %}Before closing the final open Issue, verify every story acceptance criterion and create any required follow-up Issue.{% endif %}

Parallel writable agents use one branch and one Git worktree per task, and only
independent tasks may run concurrently. Reuse a host-managed worktree when
present and never force one branch into multiple worktrees. Worktrees isolate
files, not review or integration.

If an authorized change or `copier update` alters an Issue or PR body shape,
migrate existing bodies before closing its Issue or create and link a follow-up
Issue first. For repository adoption and updates, follow the README lifecycle
contract; do not infer historical grouping or rewrite custom descriptions.

## Commands

- Required final check: `./scripts/verify`.
{% if language in ["python", "python-typescript"] %}- Python setup: `uv sync --locked`.
- Python iteration: `uv run ruff check <paths>`, `uv run mypy`, and
  `uv run pytest <test-path>`.
{% endif %}{% if language in ["typescript", "python-typescript"] %}- TypeScript setup: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
- TypeScript iteration: `pnpm exec biome check <paths>`,
  `pnpm exec tsc --noEmit`, and `pnpm exec vitest run <test-path>`.
{% endif %}- Site check:
  `uv run --no-project python scripts/render_site.py --check`.
- Targeted commands speed up iteration but never replace the final check.

## Actions quota fallback

- Use the fallback only after a human maintainer with billing visibility
  confirms that the current period's included GitHub Actions minutes are
  exhausted. Payment, budget, platform, workflow, permission, unknown, and
  started-job failures remain blocked.
- Follow `docs/ci-policy.md#actions-quota-fallback` exactly. Its SHA-bound local
  verification, attestation, human authorization, non-local control, and later
  hosted-check requirements are mandatory.

## Editing boundaries

{% if language == "ci" %}- This profile has no product language module. Add product code only after an intentional profile change.
{% else %}- Product code under {% if language in ["python", "python-typescript"] %}`src/` and `tests/`{% endif %}{% if language == "python-typescript" %}, plus {% endif %}{% if language in ["typescript", "python-typescript"] %}`typescript/src/` and `typescript/tests/`{% endif %} belongs to this project; Copier updates must not overwrite it.
- Generate lockfiles with {% if language in ["python", "python-typescript"] %}uv{% endif %}{% if language == "python-typescript" %} or {% endif %}{% if language in ["typescript", "python-typescript"] %}pnpm{% endif %}; never hand-edit integrity metadata.
{% endif %}- Keep third-party GitHub Actions pinned to full commit SHAs.
- Use local temporary projects or this repository for validation; do not create
  a separate GitHub repository.
- Write source-code comments in English. Keep style details in executable
  configuration instead of duplicating them here.

## Platform capability and automation

- Keep CI/CD behavior declarative and tested. Do not assume organization or
  enterprise administrator access.
- The portable baseline must not require a GitHub App, PAT, copied personal
  token, populated `.env`, or organization-policy change. Never persist
  `gh auth token` as project configuration.
- Represent capabilities as allowed, blocked, or unknown. Select the strongest
  safe supported mode and state any artifact-only or verification-only
  fallback; never report a skipped or blocked operation as successful.
- Pull request titles declare SemVer intent; assign exact versions only from
  merged default-branch history. Keep registry and cloud trust bootstrap
  optional and verifiable.

## Safety

- Never commit secrets, tokens, private keys, or populated `.env` files. Use
  GitHub Secrets or approved secret management for automation.
- Review `scripts/apply-repository-settings.sh plan` before `apply`; do not
  operate production or external infrastructure without explicit authorization.
- Do not bypass failed CI, required checks, resolved-conversation requirements,
  CODEOWNER review, or human approval except for the exact quota fallback above.
  It never waives human authorization or a non-local control.

## Code Review Rules

- Verify that the change satisfies Issue acceptance criteria and has relevant
  tests.
- Flag unintended public API or release changes; require an explicit
  compatibility and SemVer decision.
- Review dependencies, workflow permissions, secret handling, and artifact
  verification as security-sensitive.
- Prefer existing patterns and focused changes over new abstractions or rewrites.

## Nested instructions

- Add a nested `AGENTS.md` only for genuinely different commands, ownership,
  or safety boundaries.
- Use `AGENTS.override.md` only for intentional same-directory replacement;
  do not pre-create empty instruction files.
